CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts --verbosity 10 _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^No assigns clause provided for loop main\.1 at .*\. The inferred set \{g, main::1::2::j\} might be incomplete
^\[main.loop_assigns.\d+\] line \d+ Check assigns clause inclusion for loop main\.0: SUCCESS$
^\[main.loop_assigns.\d+\] line \d+ Check assigns clause inclusion for loop main\.1: SUCCESS$
^\[main.loop_invariant_base.\d+\] line \d+ Check invariant before entry for loop main\.0: SUCCESS$
^\[main.loop_invariant_base.\d+\] line \d+ Check invariant before entry for loop main\.1: SUCCESS$
^\[main.loop_invariant_step.\d+\] line \d+ Check invariant after step for loop main\.0: SUCCESS$
^\[main.loop_invariant_step.\d+\] line \d+ Check invariant after step for loop main\.1: SUCCESS$
^\[incr.assigns.\d+\] .* Check that \*p is assignable: SUCCESS$
^\[add_two.assigns.\d+\] .* Check that g is assignable: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* x counts the iterations: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* g counts twice the iterations: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.\d+\] .* x not written by the second loop: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^Skipping loop assigns inference
^No assigns clause provided for loop main\.0
--
This test checks that the loop assigns inference still runs when one loop has
an explicit assigns clause and the other has none, and that it infers {j, g}
for the second loop.
